---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { github } from '../lib/links';
---

<Page
  title="Security"
  description="How Scalar approaches security, and how to report a vulnerability."
  path="/security"
  eyebrow="Security"
  lede="Scalar holds OAuth tokens for your most important accounts and can act on your behalf. Security is treated as architecture, not cleanup."
>
  <h2>Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
  <p>
    Please report privately through GitHub Security Advisories on the affected repository. Do not
    open a public issue. The full policy, response targets and safe harbour terms are in the
    <a href={github.security}>security policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What the design commits to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Sessions and sign-in links are random tokens stored only as hashes. A leaked database does not
      yield usable sessions.
    </li>
    <li>OAuth refresh tokens are encrypted at rest and isolated behind a credential service.</li>
    <li>Provider secrets never reach clients. Tokens are never logged.</li>
    <li>
      Every request establishes who is asking, which workspace owns the resource, and whether that
      user may touch it. Authorization is enforced in the API, never only in the interface.
    </li>
    <li>
      Minimum OAuth scopes, requested when the corresponding feature is enabled, and explained in
      plain language.
    </li>
    <li>
      AI actions run through explicit tools with their own authorization and audit trail. The model
      never touches the database, and content from email or other external sources cannot change
      what Scalar does.
    </li>
    <li>
      Anything that writes to another system requires your approval unless you have deliberately
      turned on that automation.
    </li>
    <li>
      Changes to auth, tokens, permissions or AI actions get a dedicated security review before
      merge.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Supply chain</h2>
  <p>
    Dependabot, dependency review, secret scanning and CodeQL run across the organisation. Package
    installs enforce a minimum release age so brand new versions cannot land in a build unnoticed.
  </p>
</Page>
